# Section 4, "Obscurations and Apertures": NEW entering the section's three examples, each as element 1
# of the Cassegrain of the NEW tutorial (s04_new_cass.jou) in place of its SecMirObs: (1) Sq_Ap, a
# Flat Obscuring element at z=-5.4 with a Rectangular aperture -1.5,1.5,-1.5,1.5 and no obscurations;
# (2) 4CircObs, a Conic Obscuring element at z=-.01 with the primary's f=5.4, e=1, a Circular aperture
# 2,0,0 and four Circle pads 0.2 at (+-1,+-1); (3) SpiderObs, a Flat Obscuring element at z=-5.4 with a
# Circular aperture 2,0,0, a Circle 0.5,0,0 and two Rectangle spider arms.  The element answers are
# 3.2's.  3.2 printed only the element's dialog (Sq_Ap from its name prompt, the other two from "Enter
# Element 1 Data:") and then the element's prescription lines; NEW needs a complete deck, so the source
# and elements 2-5 (the tutorial's Primary, Secondary, Ref_surf, foc_pln) are entered as setup and not
# printed, and the prescription lines are SHOW's.  Differences from 3.2's dialog: NEW prints the element
# back and asks "Is this correct?" (default YES); the element, surface and obscuration type lists are
# longer; the aperture defaults follow the source aperture ([2.00000,0,0] and [-2,2,-2,2]; 3.2 printed
# 0.5 and +-0.5); the obscuration prompts print "y[no default]" with no blank; xObs is still asked
# when nObs is 0; the element lists xObs before nObs (3.2: after the obscurations).  NONE turns
# plotting off, so the three OPD traces draw nothing; each pins the rays the element obscures.
cd $TMP
